{"name":"Acapulco Cliffs","hex":"#4e9aa8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cliffs","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of cliff"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliffs"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acapulco","blocks":[{"text":"Acapulco de Juárez, commonly called Acapulco, is a city and major seaport in the state of Guerrero on the Pacific coast of Mexico, 380 kilometres (240 mi) south of Mexico City. Located on a deep, semicircular bay, Acapulco has been a port since the early colonial period in Mexico's history. It is a port of call for shipping and cruise lines running between Panama and San Francisco, California. The city of Acapulco is the largest in the state, larger than the state capital Chilpancingo. Acapulco is also Mexico's largest beach and balneario resort city. Acapulco de Juárez, Guerrero is the municipal seat of the municipality of Acapulco, Guerrero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg/330px-Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acapulco","wikidata":"Q81398"},{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff","wikidata":"Q107679"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/1280px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcapulco3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/500px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/1280px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg/500px-LA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_%283099407706%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALA_DIANA_CAZADORA_DE_ACAPULCO_(3099407706).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_de_la_Soledad%2C_Acapulco%2C_Guerrero_%2824297257883%29.jpg/500px-Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_de_la_Soledad%2C_Acapulco%2C_Guerrero_%2824297257883%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_de_la_Soledad%2C_Acapulco%2C_Guerrero_%2824297257883%29.jpg/1280px-Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_de_la_